// ============================================================================
// collatz-steps.epher -- how many steps to reach 1, the stopping time
//
// For a starting n, the Collatz sequence halves even values and sends odd
// values to 3n + 1. The stopping time is the number of steps until the
// sequence first reaches 1. A packed while loop (guide 1.30) runs the
// sequence; the loop is silent, so labelled prints show the counts. The
// classic checks: 27 takes 111 steps, and 13 takes 9 (13, 40, 20, 10, 5,
// 16, 8, 4, 2, 1).
//
// Source: the Collatz conjecture (the 3n + 1 problem), posed by Lothar
// Collatz in 1937; see Lagarias (ed.), "The Ultimate Challenge: The 3x+1
// Problem", AMS, 2010. The conjecture that every starting value reaches
// 1 is unproved but checked to enormous heights; every value below stops.
//
// Run it from a terminal:  epher "epher scripts/number-theory/integer-sequences/collatz-steps.epher"
// In the REPL:             load epher scripts/number-theory/integer-sequences/collatz-steps.epher
// Web/desktop:             paste the whole file into the entry, press Enter
//
// There is no knob: the starting values 27 and 13 are the checks.
// ============================================================================

// ---- one Collatz step ----------------------------------------------------------------
def step(n) = if mod(n, 2) == 0 then n / 2 else 3 * n + 1

// ---- stopping time of 27 ---------------------------------------------------------------
// v = {current value, steps}; the loop ends when the value reaches 1.
v = {27, 0}
while v[1] != 1 do v = {step(v[1]), v[2] + 1}
print("stopping time of 27:", v[2])         // the classic 111 steps

// ---- stopping time of 13 ---------------------------------------------------------------
// The same loop shape for a smaller start, checked by hand above.
v = {13, 0}
while v[1] != 1 do v = {step(v[1]), v[2] + 1}
print("stopping time of 13:", v[2])         // 9 steps
print("sequence of 13:", 13, 40, 20, 10, 5, 16, 8, 4, 2, 1)
// ---- expected output ----
// = {27, 0}
// = stopping time of 27: 111
// = {13, 0}
// = stopping time of 13: 9
// = sequence of 13: 13 40 20 10 5 16 8 4 2 1
